# Source: Exercise list "ICT3101 Exercises - Deductive Verification" (file "HoareLogic Exercises.pdf"), problem 3 c), with its solution sheet.
# The exercise asks for the precondition. The precondition given here is the answer, x = y.
id: conditional-min
title: A conditional with a minimum postcondition
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Exercise list "HoareLogic Exercises", problem 3 c
tags: conditional, wp

task:
  Calculate the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition
  $Q$: first the weakest precondition of each branch, then the one of the
  conditional. Then use the consequence rule to check that the
  precondition $P$ implies it.

given:
  Condition B: $x - y > 0$

pre: x = y
post: z <= y && z <= x

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  if (x - y > 0) then {
    {R1}
    z := x            // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {R2}
    z := y            // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of the then branch z := x
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
text:
  Each branch ends where the conditional ends, so its postcondition is
  $Q$. We substitute the assigned variable ($z$) with the assignment
  expression ($x$) in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(z := x, Q)
  = (z <= y && z <= x)[z <- x]
  = x <= y && x <= x        // every z is replaced by x
  = x <= y && true          // x <= x holds for every x
  = x <= y                  // A && true is A
set: R1 = x <= y

step: R2, the weakest precondition of the else branch z := y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($z$) with the assignment
  expression ($y$) in the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(z := y, Q)
  = (z <= y && z <= x)[z <- y]
  = y <= y && y <= x        // every z is replaced by y
  = true && y <= x          // y <= y holds for every y
  = y <= x                  // true && A is A
set: R2 = y <= x

step: R3, the weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: IF, WP
focus: if, R1, R2
text:
  The weakest precondition of `if B then c1 else c2` for $Q$ is
  >> (B ==> wp(c1, Q)) && (!B ==> wp(c2, Q))
  Here the condition $B$ is $x - y > 0$, $wp(c1, Q)$ is $R1$ and
  $wp(c2, Q)$ is $R2$:
  >>> («x - y > 0 : B» ==> «R1») && («!(x - y > 0) : ¬B» ==> «R2»)
calc:
  wp(if (x - y > 0) then z := x else z := y, Q)
  = (x - y > 0 ==> x <= y) && (!(x - y > 0) ==> y <= x)   // B, R1 and R2 put in the formula
  = (x > y ==> x <= y) && (!(x > y) ==> y <= x)           // x - y > 0 is x > y (add y to both sides)
  = (x > y ==> x <= y) && (x <= y ==> y <= x)             // !(x > y) is x <= y
  = x <= y && (x <= y ==> y <= x)                         // x <= y is !(x > y), and A ==> !A is !A
  = x <= y && y <= x                                      // A && (A ==> B) is A && B
  = x = y                                                 // x <= y and y <= x together say x = y
set: R3 = x = y

step: P implies R3
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R3
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R3»
vc: x = y ==> x = y
why:
  $P$ and $R3$ are the same formula, and every formula implies itself.
result: The triple is valid, and $x = y$ is the weakest precondition.

summary:
  The program stores in $z$ the larger of $x$ and $y$, but $Q$ asks for
  a value that is at most $x$ and at most $y$. The larger value is also
  the smaller one only when $x = y$.
